{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1470079-gods-in-changeling","id":1470079,"title":"Gods in Changeling?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-12-09T15:19:47-08:00","id":1470079,"title":"Gods in Changeling?","content":"After rereading American Gods for the nth time, I have started to think about the paralells between Gaiman's gods (with their focus on faith in them, on stories, symbols, roles and patterns) and Changeling's characters (which, if you look beyond the &quot;dreams&quot; thing, are also very much about stories and roles, about belief in something greater and wondrous - although this might be just my interpretation). Did anybody ever try to combine those two settings, or to bring godly beings to Changeling in a different way? I know that Gallain, especially Hsien, are quite close to that in many respects, but I'd really love to know whether somebody tried to reinterpret dieties as beings as much dependent on humanity's dreams, wonder, awe and storytelling as the fae, to make them less Umbrood and more beings of the Dreaming? And if so, how did it work out?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24670-ufnal","name":"Ufnal","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-09T16:56:27-08:00","id":1470088,"title":"","content":"If you read Kith book Satyr, you'll see that in cannon the Greek Gods at least were powerful fay. In real history, many Europeans, Milton, Nicolas of Cusa, Petrarch, Roger Bacon, among others, believed that most of the pagan gods of Europe were fairies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-09T17:26:24-08:00","id":1470091,"title":"","content":"The default Changeling setting definitely uses real world legend and theology in several places. As mentioned above the Satyr Kith book describes the Olympian gods as existing with similar allusions to Norse gods in the Troll kithbook iirc and of course the Tuatha de Danaan themselves take their name from the Pre-Christian Celtic gods of Ireland. So the gods definitely have some established presence in Changeling lore.<br /> <br /> How I personally handle things like this is to take basically a two-pronged approach from Changeling specific lore and then broad WoD lore applications - what follows are my interpretations, head canons, and personal methods:<br /> <br /> Contemporary Changelings are the descendants of the early Fae spirits who have gone through the Changeling Way. Kithain are Fae of the Dream who have followed the Firstborn since they first arose. Thallain are Fae of Nightmare who have followed the Elder Dark since they first arose. The Elder Dark formed first from the gestalt mass of pre-enlightened human and animal passions such as fear, hunger, aggression, and lust. After human enlightenment the Firstborn arose from the passions which followed which carried dreams of loyalty, security, creativity, beauty, leadership, joy, etc. They\u2019ve fought ever since, alongside the Inanimae born of the Dreams of Sleeping Things (Plants, the elements, inanimate objects, etc). This can be applied as a general schema to the figures of many theologies. For Celtic theology you have the Tuatha de Danaan as the Firstborn and the Fomorians as the Elder Dark with various Inanimae allies on either side. For Greek theology you have the Olympians as the Firstborn and the Titans as the Elder Dark and Primordial Incarnae as well. Not every theology will be able to neatly click into this schema and so it must be considered on a case by case basis but it is generally useful.<br /> <br /> Looking more broadly at the place of the divine in the World of Darkness we can look to a few places. While obviously various splats have members who claim to be gods but I generally have those either be delusional individuals, manipulative cult leaders, or those who have adopted monikers for whatever personal reasoning. That said we have seven main places to look for the divine in WoD, in my mind. Let\u2019s start with the first three - the Umbra. The High Umbra is the realm of thought (concepts, abstractions, philosophy, forms) and so there you will find beings who represent the ideas of every god and spirit ever imagined. They are that being, to be sure, but specifically they are human ideas about those beings. The Middle Umbra is the realm of passion (experience, instinct, animism, nature) and so there what you will find are beings who can be made to wear the masks or names or gods because they represent essences of that being as it\u2019s experienced by humanity. You may indeed find Dionysus in the Middle Unbra, although what you speak to is more a powerful spirit of intoxicated wisdom and freedom from inhibition, wearing the god like a mask. The Lower Umbra is the realm of memory (memorial, recollected, cthonic) and so while you plunge into those depths you may come across shades of the departed who yet remain within our memory or at least their own, in Far Shores or deep cthonic realms you may find spirits who embody cthonic gods or at least claim too to appease uneasy spirits. The Dreaming is the realm of human dreams and so the gods walk there like they do in human dreams, powerful but outside of the Deep Dreaming they resemble more their legendary selves from folklore and story rather then the gods of cultic rites and the mysteries of faith. I would keep them bound generally to the Far Dreaming with servants or lesser incarnations to be found in the Near Dreaming and more esoteric, perhaps even Jungian, vessels of divine dreams to be found in the Far Dreaming. You also might find gods who behave akin to Bygones, no longer accepted as true amongst most of humanity, and so living an existence on the fringe of reality as they try to remain connected to humanity no matter how dubiously. You may also find the gods in an unseen way, their power shining out from humans with True Faith, the gods writing themselves on the collective symbols of human faith and belief and being championed by individuals with the power to manifest that belief. The most esoteric answer about where you might find the gods in the World or Darkness is with The One, a gathering of unseen and mysterious beings who cannot be fully comprehended by humanity and yet exist by the cause of themselves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-10T06:29:44-08:00","id":1470128,"title":"","content":"Ah, yes! This is something that I love to insert into my Changeling games. Though I keep such elements in the background rather than front and center. Some deities, like those found among the many lesser gods, goddesses, and folk saints, can easily be Changelings or Dark Kin, while other deities might easily be fae beings or Umbrood.<br /> <br /> Some entities that frequent my games are as follows:<br /> <br /> <b>Mr. Appleby </b><br /> Mr. Applebee is the powerful City Father totem of New York City, and a powerful patron to fae and mortals. This entity appears as a tough as nails businessman with a big round face and hands that are full of rings. This entity dwells primarily in the Near Dreaming of NYC, ruling over its many dreams and keeping an eye on the fae and other supernatural beings who dwell there. He is served by his five sons (The Borough Boys), who keep an eye on the various aspects of the cities supernatural and mortal communities. <br /> <br /> <b>Euryale</b><br /> Euryale, also called the Far Springer, is one of the two Immortal Gorgons, and one of three vicious sisters with brass hands, sharp fangs, and hair made of living, venomous snakes. Like her sisters, she is able to turn any creature into stone by her gaze. She sees Man as the weaker sex, and deserves to be subjugated by Woman. When walking among mortals, Euryale appears as a sturdy &quot;punk rocker&quot; woman with thick hair and a permanently sour expression.<br /> Euryale has frequently been seen in <a href=\"http://www.ottosshrunkenhead.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Otto's Shrunken Head</a> in Alphabet City NYC. She has been known to &quot;incite&quot; more than a few Redcap and Ogre women to exert their power.<br /> <br /> <b>Saint Grobian</b><br /> Saint Grobian (Sanctus Grobianus) is the patron saint of vulgar and rude people. He appears most often as a shockingly dirty and disgusting person that somehow manages to turn up at social gatherings. While present, the Saint will eat and drink to excess, belch, fart and laugh uproariously, even in the face of enemies (especially when around enemies).<br /> <br /> <b>Irusan, the King of Cats</b><br /> Also known as the &quot;King of Cats,&quot; Irusan is an enigmatic figure. He typically appears in the form of a giant, black cat that lurks in labyrinthine alleys, warehouses and other dark places. The King of Cats is a dapper fellow with a sharp sword and a razor wit. Although his dress changes to suit the occasion, he seems to prefer swashbuckler trappings. Though he turns feline if need be (sometimes white, often black), the King of Cats prefers to dance around in human guise, daring enemies to harm him. He is the soul of valiant rebellion, and favors those who live and die with style.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">\u201cHumpty had always sat on walls, it was his way.\u201d </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">\u2015 </span></span></span><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Lato\"><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Jasper Fforde, </span></b></span></span><span style=\"color:#181818\"><span style=\"font-family:Merriweather\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/work/quotes/2504943\" target=\"_blank\">The Big Over Easy</a></span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":1756,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1756-mythadvocate","user":"MythAdvocate","date":". 12-10-2021, 03:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1756-mythadvocate","name":"MythAdvocate","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"444"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}